    monitoring: "Paracetamol level, ALT, AST, INR, glucose, bicarbonate, lactate q8-12h until stable. Signs of hepatotoxicity: ALT rising (peak day 3-4), INR prolonged, hypoglycaemia, encephalopathy, metabolic acidosis. If INR >2.0 or ALT >1000: continue NAC beyond 21 hours. If severe hepatotoxicity: discuss with transplant centre (King's College Criteria: pH <7.3 OR all three of INR >6.5, Cr >3.4 mg/dL, Grade III-IV encephalopathy).",
    counselling: "Paracetamol overdose can cause liver failure that may not be noticeable for 24-72 hours. You have received the antidote which will protect your liver. You will need to stay on the infusion for 21 hours. We will monitor your blood tests regularly. We also need to address the reasons behind this — a psychiatrist will speak with you.",
    followUp: "Medical: Complete NAC course. Discharge when: LFTs improving/improved, INR normalising, no encephalopathy. Psychiatric: Before discharge, must be assessed by psychiatrist — do NOT discharge without psychiatric clearance. Follow-up LFTs in 1 week.",
    pearls: "NAC is the antidote of choice — it works by replenishing hepatic glutathione stores. It is most effective within 8 hours of ingestion (almost 100% protective). Paracetamol is metabolised primarily by glucuronidation and sulphation; a small fraction goes through CYP2E1 to form NAPQI (toxic metabolite). In overdose, glucuronidation/sulphation pathways become saturated, and NAPQI accumulates. Glutathione can detoxify up to ~5g paracetamol — beyond that, liver injury occurs. The Rumack-Matthew nomogram is used for timing of ingestion and need for NAC. In resource-limited settings, if paracetamol levels cannot be obtained: give NAC if ingestion >150 mg/kg or >7.5g total. Single acute ingestions >150 mg/kg are considered potentially toxic.",
    references: ["Kenya Clinical Guidelines: Paracetamol Overdose", "TOXBASE: Paracetamol Poisoning", "WHO: Poisoning Management"],
